      "parent_index": null,
      "last_updated": "2026-05-12T00:00:00.000000",
      "title_align": "left",
      "section": "About",
      "order": 6,
      "alignment": "left",
      "vertical_alignment": "top",
      "body": "A whole HeLa cell imaged by focused ion beam scanning electron microscopy (FIB-SEM): 12000 x 1600 x 6368 voxels at 4 x 4 x 5.24 nm, about 122 GB, in six levels from 4 to 128 nm per pixel. It is read in place from Janelia's public OpenOrganelle bucket (jrc_hela-2, CC BY 4.0): each tile only fetches the chunks it shows, so zooming from the cell to a single mitochondrion downloads megabytes, not the volume. Experts annotated organelles voxel by voxel in 26 small crops of the cell; the boxes on the overview are those crops, coloured by the organelle that fills most of each. Click a crop (or draw a rectangle over several) and the view below zooms to it at full resolution and moves to its depth; the plots and the table follow. The crop table is built from the crops' metadata alone (dev/bioimage/make_cosem_crops.py); no voxel is copied. Data: Xu et al., Nature 599, 147-151 (2021) and Heinrich et al., Nature 599, 141-146 (2021).\n",
      "surface": "none",
      "accent": null,
      "logo": null,
      "logo_dark": null,
      "wf_tag": "python/bioimage_demo"
    },
